AB  - Mental health disorders create profound personal and societal burdens, yet conventional diagnostics are resource-intensive and limit accessibility. Recent  advances in artificial intelligence, particularly natural language processing and  multimodal methods, offer promise for detecting and addressing mental disorders.  However, these innovations also introduce privacy concerns. Here we examine these  challenges and propose solutions, including anonymization, synthetic data and  privacy-preserving training, while outlining frameworks for privacy-utility  trade-offs, aiming to advance reliable, privacy-aware artificial-intelligence  tools that support clinical decision-making and improve mental health outcomes.
DO  - 10.1038/s43588-025-00875-w
