AB  - Major Depressive Disorder (MDD) is an affective disorder that can lead to persistent sadness and a decline in the quality of life, increasing the risk of  suicide. Utilizing multimodal data such as electroencephalograms and patient  interview audios can facilitate the timely detection of MDD. However, existing  depression detection methods either consider only a single modality or do not  fully account for the differences and similarities between modalities in  multimodal approaches, potentially overlooking the latent information inherent in  various modal data. To address these challenges, we propose EMO-GCN, a multimodal  depression detection method based on an adaptive multi-graph neural network. By  employing graph-based methods to model data from various modalities and  extracting features from them, the potential correlations between modalities are  uncovered. The model's performance on the MODMA dataset is outstanding, achieving  an accuracy (ACC) of 96.30%. Ablation studies further confirm the effectiveness  of the model's individual components.The experimental results of EMO-GCN  demonstrate the application prospects of graph-based multimodal analysis in the  field of mental health, offering new perspectives for future research.
DO  - 10.1038/s41598-024-79981-0
