AB  - OBJECTIVES: This study compared machine learning models using unimodal imaging measures and combined multi-modal imaging measures for deep brain stimulation  (DBS) outcome prediction in treatment resistant depression (TRD). METHODS:  Regional brain glucose metabolism (CMRGlu), cerebral blood flow (CBF), and grey  matter volume (GMV) were measured at baseline using 18F-fluorodeoxy glucose  (18F-FDG) positron emission tomography (PET), arterial spin labelling (ASL)  magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and T1-weighted MRI, respectively, in 19  patients with TRD receiving subcallosal cingulate (SCC)-DBS. Responders (n = 9)  were defined by a 50% reduction in HAMD-17 at 6 months from the baseline. Using  an atlas-based approach, values of each measure were determined for pre-selected  brain regions. OneR feature selection algorithm and the naïve Bayes model was  used for classification. Leave-out-one cross validation was used for classifier  evaluation. RESULTS: The performance accuracy of the CMRGlu classification model  (84%) was greater than CBF (74%) or GMV (74%) models. The classification model  using the three image modalities together led to a similar accuracy (84%0  compared to the CMRGlu classification model. CONCLUSIONS: CMRGlu imaging measures  may be useful for the development of multivariate prediction models for SCC-DBS  studies for TRD. The future of multivariate methods for multimodal imaging may  rest on the selection of complementing features and the developing better  models.Clinical Trial Registration: ClinicalTrials.gov (#NCT01983904).
