AB  - Graph neural networks (GNNs) have shown potential in analyzing brain functional networks for neuropsychiatric disorder diagnosis, yet existing GNN-based methods  for attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) identification face accuracy  limitations due to inadequate multi-modal integration. To address this problem,  we propose a graph attention convolutional network (Att-GCN) that combines  multi-band amplitude of low-frequency fluctuations (ALFF) and functional  connectivity (FC) data within a unified graph representation. The framework  constructs an original graph with ALFF as nodes and FC as edges, then converts FC  data into supplementary nodes while preserving their topological associations  with ALFF nodes. This transformation yields a dual graph in which both modalities  participate directly in graph learning. A graph-based feature selection approach  is applied to refine the dual graph and retain the most informative features. Our  Att-GCN network learns high-level features on this refined dual graph and  identifies ADHD subjects within a binary hypothesis testing framework.  Experimental results demonstrate an average accuracy of 93.1% on various ADHD-200  datasets, specifically within the right limbic system containing only 11 brain  regions. Our found biomarkers are reliable and located around the thalamus and  hippocampus, which are highly correlated with ADHD.
DO  - 10.1016/j.compbiolchem.2025.108866
