AB  - Sexual sadism is an extreme, pathological manifestation of sadistic traits. While the psychological and forensic characteristics of sexual sadism have been  extensively studied, the underlying neurocircuitry is far from being understood.  In this study, n = 232 incarcerated men completed structural MRI scans, n = 116  of whom had been convicted of sexual offenses. Sexual offenders were assessed for  the presence and severity of sadism using the Sexual Sadism Scale (SeSaS).  Multivariate (source-based morphometry (SBM)) and univariate (voxel-based  morphometry (VBM)) techniques were used to identify neural networks and  individual regions in which gray matter volume was related to sexual sadism. Both  SBM and VBM revealed associations between SeSaS scores and posterior temporal  regions, including the temporoparietal junction (TPJ) and fusiform gyrus, two  regions critical for social cognition and processing high-order visual  information, respectively. Men with high SeSaS scores indicative of sexual sadism  had enlarged gray matter volume in these regions, relative to both men with low  SeSaS scores and men who had not committed sexual offenses. One of the temporal  regions (right tTPJ) overlapped closely with the rTPJ region previously  implicated in a functional MRI study of pain processing in sexual sadism.  Overall, the results provide multi-modal evidence of the relevance of temporal  cortical networks to sexual sadism.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jpsychires.2025.12.021
