AB  - There is an increasing role for biological markers (biomarkers) in the understanding and diagnosis of neurodegenerative disorders. The application of  imaging biomarkers specifically for the in vivo investigation of  neurodegenerative disorders has increased substantially over the past decades and  continues to provide further benefits both to the diagnosis and understanding of  these diseases. This review forms part of a series of articles which stem from  the University College London/University of Gothenburg course "Biomarkers in  neurodegenerative diseases". In this review, we focus on neuroimaging,  specifically positron emission tomography (PET) and magnetic resonance imaging  (MRI), giving an overview of the current established practices clinically and in  research as well as new techniques being developed. We will also discuss the use  of machine learning (ML) techniques within these fields to provide additional  insights to early diagnosis and multimodal analysis.
DO  - 10.1186/s13195-020-00612-7
