AB  - Early intervention is now considered the core treatment strategy for autism spectrum disorders (ASD). Thus, it is of significant clinical importance to  establish a screening tool for the early detection of ASD in infants. To achieve  this goal, in a longitudinal design, we analyzed spontaneous bodily movements of  4-month-old infants from general population and assessed their ASD-like behaviors  at 18 months of age. A total of 26 movement features were calculated from  video-recorded bodily movements of infants at 4 months of age. Their risk of ASD  was assessed at 18 months of age with the Modified Checklist for Autism in  Toddlerhood, a widely used screening questionnaire. Infants at high risk for ASD  at 18 months of age exhibited less rhythmic and weaker bodily movement patterns  at 4 months of age than low-risk infants. When the observed bodily movement  patterns were submitted to a machine learning-based analysis, linear and  non-linear classifiers successfully predicted ASD-like behavior at 18 months of  age based on the bodily movement patterns at 4 months of age, at the level  acceptable for practical use. This study analyzed the relationship between  spontaneous bodily movements at 4 months of age and the ASD risk at 18 months of  age. Experimental results suggested the utility of the proposed method for the  early screening of infants at risk for ASD. We revealed that the signs of ASD  risk could be detected as early as 4 months after birth, by focusing on the  infant's spontaneous bodily movements.
DO  - 10.1038/s41598-022-21308-y
