AB  - Humans are a highly social species. Complex interactions for mutual support range from helping neighbors to building social welfare institutions. During times of  distress or crisis, sharing life experiences within one's social circle is  critical for well-being. By translating pattern-learning algorithms to the UK  Biobank imaging-genetics cohort (n = ~40 000 participants), we have delineated  manifestations of regular social support in multimodal whole-brain measurements.  In structural brain variation, we identified characteristic volumetric signatures  in the salience and limbic networks for high- versus low-social support  individuals. In patterns derived from functional coupling, we also located  interindividual differences in social support in action-perception circuits  related to binding sensory cues and initiating behavioral responses. In line with  our demographic profiling analysis, the uncovered neural substrates have  potential implications for loneliness, substance misuse, and resilience to  stress.
DO  - 10.1093/cercor/bhab109
