AB  - Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a progressive neurodegenerative disease with phenotypic changes closely associated with both genetic variants and imaging  pathology. Brain imaging biomarker genomics has been developed in recent years to  reveal potential AD pathological mechanisms and provide early diagnoses. This  technique integrates multimodal imaging phenotypes with genetic data in a  noninvasive and high-throughput manner. In this review, we summarize the basic  analytical framework of brain imaging biomarker genomics and elucidate two main  implementation scenarios of this technique in AD studies: (1) exploring novel  biomarkers and seeking mutual interpretability and (2) providing a diagnosis and  prognosis for AD with combined use of machine learning methods and brain imaging  biomarker genomics. Importantly, we highlight the necessity of brain imaging  biomarker genomics, discuss the strengths and limitations of current methods, and  propose directions for development of this research field.
DO  - 10.1186/s40035-022-00315-z
