AB  - The urea-cycle disorders (UCDs) are a group of congenital enzyme and carrier deficiencies predisposing to hyperammonemia (HA). HA causes changes in the  central nervous system (CNS) including alterations of neurotransmitter function,  cell volume, and energy deprivation ultimately leading to cerebral edema.  Neuropathological findings of UCDs primarily reflect changes in astrocyte  morphology. Neurological features accompanying acute HA include changes in  behavior and consciousness in the short term, and potential for impairments in  memory and executive function as long-term effects. Plasma measures of ammonia  and glutamine, although useful for clinical monitoring, prove poor markers of CNS  function. Multimodal neuroimaging has potential to investigate impact on  cognitive function by interrogating neural networks, connectivity and  biochemistry. As neuroimaging methods become increasingly sophisticated, they  will play a critical role in clinical monitoring and treatment of metabolic  disease. We describe our findings in UCDs; with focus on Ornithine  Transcarbamylase deficiency (OTCD) the only X linked UCD.
DO  - 10.1007/s11011-012-9348-0
