AB  - BACKGROUND: Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a neurodegenerative disorder characterized by deposition of amyloid plaques and disruption of neural  circuitry, leading to cognitive decline. Animal models of AD deposit senile  plaques and exhibit structural and functional deficits in neurons and neural  networks. An effective treatment would prevent or restore these deficits,  including calcium dyshomeostasis observed with in-vivo imaging. METHODS: We  examined the effects of DA-9803, a multimodal botanical drug, in 5XFAD and  APP/PS1 transgenic mice which underwent daily oral treatment with 30 or 100 mg/kg  DA-9803 or vehicle alone. Behavioral testing and longitudinal imaging of amyloid  deposits and intracellular calcium in neurons with multiphoton microscopy was  performed. RESULTS: Chronic administration of DA-9803 restored behavioral  deficits in 5XFAD mice and reduced amyloid-β levels. DA-9803 also prevented  progressive amyloid plaque deposition in APP/PS1 mice. Elevated calcium, detected  in a subset of neurons before the treatment, was restored and served as a  functional indicator of treatment efficacy in addition to the behavioral readout.  In contrast, mice treated with vehicle alone continued to progressively  accumulate amyloid plaques and calcium overload. CONCLUSIONS: In summary,  treatment with DA-9803 prevented structural and functional outcome measures in  mouse models of AD. Thus, DA-9803 shows promise as a novel therapeutic approach  for Alzheimer's disease.
DO  - 10.1186/s13195-018-0338-2
