AB  - Significant clinical symptoms of Cohen syndrome (CS), a rare autosomal recessive disorder, include intellectual disability, facial dysmorphism, postnatal  microcephaly, retinal dystrophy, and intermittent neutropenia. CS has been  associated with mutations in the VPS13B (vacuolar protein sorting 13 homolog B)  gene, which regulates vesicle-mediated protein sorting and transport; however,  the cellular mechanism underlying CS pathogenesis in patient-derived neurons  remains uncertain. This report states that autophagic vacuoles accumulate in CS  fibroblasts and the axonal terminals of CS patient-specific induced pluripotent  stem cells (CS iPSC)-derived neurons; additionally, autophagic flux was  significantly increased in CS-derived neurons compared to control neurons. VPS13B  knockout HeLa cell lines generated using the CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing system  showed significant upregulation of autophagic flux, indicating that VSP13B may be  associated with autophagy in CS. Transcriptomic analysis focusing on the  autophagy pathway revealed that genes associated with autophagosome organization  were dysregulated in CS-derived neurons. ATG4C is a mammalian ATG4 paralog and a  crucial regulatory component of the autophagosome biogenesis/recycling pathway.  ATG4C was significantly upregulated in CS-derived neurons, indicating that  autophagy is upregulated in CS neurons. The autophagy pathway in CS neurons may  be associated with the pathophysiology exhibited in the neural network of CS  patients.
DO  - 10.1186/s13041-020-00611-7
