AB  - The present aim was to explore the possibilities of using neural networks for recognizing significant changes in electrical activity of human facial muscles.  We used multilayer perceptron neural networks to recognize bursts of  electromyographic signals recorded with bipolar surface electrodes from two  subject's facial muscles. Wavelets were applied for the detection of high  frequency components of electromyographic signals. Coefficients of wavelets were  used as an input to a neural network in order to differentiate bursts from the  signals. The results showed that the recognition of bursts was very successful  resulting to 84-97 percent total accuracies. The results were very encouraging  and suggest further that the measurement of facial muscle activity may be a  potentially useful computer input signal, for example, for affective computing  which can be seen as a future versatile interaction between the computer and the  user.
