AB  - BACKGROUND: Pediatric bipolar disorder(BD) is difficult to distinguish from other psychiatric disorders, a challenge which can result in delayed or incorrect  interventions. Understanding objective neural markers reflecting a dysfunction in  recognizing implicit facial emotional social cues may aid in early diagnosis in  BD. METHODS: We used neuroimaging and a dynamic causal modeling(DCM) framework to  identify effective connectivity(EC) measures differentiating a rarified sample of  inpatient adolescents with BD from other inpatient psychopathology(OP), and  healthy adolescents(HC) during an emotional faces processing task. We  hypothesized that EC patterns would differ between groups, reflecting  disorder-specific alterations in neural networks involved in emotion processing,  and that within inpatient groups, the observed EC patterns would be associated  with the severity of clinical mood symptoms. DCM was used to analyze EC in  BD(n = 29), OP(n = 39), and HC(n = 21); mean age 15.26 years, 60 females.  Participants performed a dynamic emotional faces paradigm with four conditions:  anger, fear, sadness, and happiness. Group-level Parametric Empirical Bayes(PEB)  analyses examined EC patterns, followed by correlation analyses of relationships  between EC and clinical symptom severity. RESULTS: Analysis revealed divergent  and convergent EC patterns across groups and conditions. EC in BD and OP was  associated with self-reported clinical symptoms severity of current mania,  current depression, positive and negative affect. CONCLUSIONS: Our findings  suggest that altered EC patterns between regions supporting social emotion  processing and executive control may underlie difficulties in implicit facial  emotion processing. These dysfunctional EC patterns may contribute to social  impairments in pediatric BD. Understanding these neural connectivity alterations  could inform targeted interventions to mitigate the adverse effects of BD on  social functioning in youth.
DO  - 10.1016/j.jad.2026.121348
