AB  - The global prevalence of depression necessitates the application of technological solutions, particularly sensor-based systems, to augment scarce resources for  early diagnostic purposes. In this study, we use benchmark datasets that contain  multimodal data including video, audio, and transcribed text. To address  depression detection as a chronic long-term disorder reflected by temporal  behavioral patterns, we propose a novel framework that segments videos into  utterance-level instances using GRU for contextual representation, and then  constructs graphs where utterance embeddings serve as nodes connected through  dual relationships capturing both chronological development and intermittent  relevant information. Graph neural networks are employed to learn  multi-dimensional edge relationships and align multimodal representations across  different temporal dependencies. Our approach achieves superior performance with  an MAE of 5.25 and RMSE of 6.75 on AVEC2014, and CCC of 0.554 and RMSE of 4.61 on  AVEC2019, demonstrating significant improvements over existing methods that focus  primarily on momentary expressions.
DO  - 10.3390/s25144520
