AB  - This paper presents a novel approach for stress classification using a Dense Residual Sparse Attention-based Multi-Attention Pooling Transformer (DRSA-MAPT). By leveraging dense residual connections, sparse attention mechanisms, and a multi-attention pooling strategy, the proposed architecture effectively captures stress-related features from physiological and behavioral data. The model also integrates advanced temporal and spatial feature extraction techniques, enabling it to analyze complex multi-modal data with high efficiency. We find that experimental results outperform current state-of-theart models with regard to of resilience and reliability. The DRSA-MAPT framework has potential applications in mental health monitoring, workplace stress analysis, and personalized healthcare systems, offering a scalable and adaptable solution for stress detection across diverse environments.
