AB  - This study addresses escalating digital fatigue and anxiety among university students by introducing a non-invasive vision attentive system that jointly detects both states from facial expressions and upper-body posture, augmented with profile features. MediaPipe and the Facial Action Coding System (FACS) were used to extract facial and body landmarks. A frame-level Feedforward Neural Network (FFNN) performed emotion recognition (95.6 %). At the same time, a Long-Term Recurrent Convolutional Network (LRCN) and FFNN integrated spatiotemporal representations with eleven profile attributes for fatigue/anxiety prediction. The hybrid model demonstrated high reliability with minimal loss. Among positive cases, 80 % of negative emotions (disgust, anger, and sadness) were observed. Time-series analyses indicated that fatigue increased during uninterrupted study and decreased rapidly after brief music breaks; distances and angles derived from landmarks emerged as the most informative biomarkers. The proposed multimodal approach enables robust, real-time monitoring and targeted wellness interventions, supporting student well-being and learning outcomes and demonstrating the scalability of landmark-based biomarkers in educational settings.
