AB  - Stress is a complex and evolving condition that influences cognitive performance, emotional stability, and overall health. Conventional detection approaches, which rely on unimodal data or static classification, often fail to capture how stress develops over time. To address this limitation, we propose ProgStress-LSTM, a multimodal framework enhanced with Natural Language Processing (NLP) to analyze stress progression. The Framework integrates sensors on three complementary dimensions: (i) physiological signals, including heart rate variability and galvanic skin response, (ii) visual signals derived from facial expressions, and (iii) personal text documents and dialogue transcripts. Each modality undergoes denoising, normalization, and other forms of feature engineering. Texts, in this case, are represented using transformer based embeddings. After feature extraction, the integrated signals are passed through deep stacked LSTMs, which capture time-related and development-based patterns. The unique NLP components also add contextuality, which helps in the variability of the sensor and language data. These are some of the many reason why in the benchmark data, ProgStress LSTM exceeds the other LSTM models, as well as non-temporal baselines, in accuracy, F1 score, and temporal consistency. Multi Stress LSTM even achieves better F1 score and temporal consistency in benchmark data. Real-time integration of the framework in systems such as mental health care, workplace wellness and even academic surveillance has great promise.
