AB  - Emotional health contributes to both mental and physical health, as an emotional imbalance can lead to cognitive difficulties. The early recognition of emotional alterations is essential for the prevention of severe mental illness and for the enhancement of healthcare. Wearable sensors are equipped with physiological signals, such as Galvanic skin response (GSR), Heart rate (HR), Skin temperature (ST), and Respiration rate (RR), which aid in accurately detecting emotions more effectively than self-reported approaches. Traditional FER models tend to be weak in interpreting contradictory and dynamic physiological signals. Many previous studies rely on minimal physiological features and have limited recognition of emotions, thus resulting in relatively low precision. In this study, we suggest a hybrid ML strategy by integrating Random Forest (RF) and XGBoost to enhance emotional classification further. The study combines multiple data sets with physiological signals and annotations of emotions, enabling a thorough evaluation. We preprocess the data by performing noise reduction, predicting missing values, and balancing the emotion labels, allowing the model to learn more effectively. We evaluate the proposed model using its precision, F1 score, classification report, and confusion matrix to study misclassifications. Experimental results show that this hybrid model not only enhances emotion recognition performance but also surpasses the current prevalent methods. This work contributes to the emerging field of emotion recognition from wearable devices, aiming for a more efficient approach for real-time mental health monitoring as well as affective computing.
