AB  - Mental illnesses, such as depression and anxiety, are emerging as an increasing global public health concern. Effective technologies to detect early phases of mental illness, as well as monitor individual symptoms over time in both public health and clinical settings, are needed. Traditional digital health technologies have users' information consolidated in a central repository, and thus may have implications for privacy and security. An alternative approach is collaborative machine learning methods such as Federated Learning (FL), which retains data on-device rather than at a central location; this preserves user's sensitive personal information. Although the data remains on-device, more efficient solutions such as sharing only model updates and aggregating computations are possible. In implementation, FL provides on-device data storage and even when sharing model updates, data privacy is maintained, while predictive accuracy may remain similar to data-driven algorithms. This review presents FedMind, a FL-based framework for preserving privacy in monitoring mental health. FedMind incorporates PHQ-9 survey scores, alongside facial emotion recognition models for capturing both subjective and behavioral signals.
