AB  - To address the issues of lag, single-dimensional data, and weak privacy protection mechanisms in current university mental health crisis early warning systems, this paper implements a student mental health crisis early warning system based on multimodal data fusion. The system comprehensively utilizes existing campus surveillance cameras, audio equipment, and social media data to construct a data collection and processing pipeline for three modalities: video (facial expressions), audio (tone and semantics), and text (social media content). DeepFace and ArcFace are employed for facial identity and expression recognition; ECAPA-TDNN and Transformer technologies are used to achieve audio speaker identification, speech emotion recognition, and text transcription; and the BERT model is utilized for sentiment analysis of the transcribed text and social media content. An innovative dynamic weighted multimodal emotion fusion algorithm is proposed, which calculates single-channel and comprehensive emotion scores and introduces a baseline emotion and fluctuation detection mechanism to achieve dynamic, quantitative assessment and abnormal early warning of students' mental states. Role-based three-tier access control and data anonymization techniques are adopted to ensure data security throughout its lifecycle. Experimental results show that the system achieved a mental health crisis identification accuracy of 91.4% in a pilot deployment, with a significantly reduced false alarm rate. This research applies deep learning and multimodal information processing technologies to the field of mental health, providing a feasible technical solution for building an intelligent and proactive campus psychological protection system.
