AB  - With the rapid growth of meme culture across social media sites, the role of memes in shaping mental health and sentiment interpretation has become a relevant research field. Nevertheless, precise categorization of the sentiment of memes has proven difficult to achieve owing to the intricate interactions between text and images. This paper delivers a comparative discussion of unimodal and multimodal methods of meme classification. The multimodal model combines ResNet101 and VGG16 for image features and BERT for textual data processing, while the unimodal models are: An image model implemented using ResNet101 and VGG16 to classify images standalone. A text model that uses BERT for text-only classification. The results from the individual models are concatenated, where the predictions from the two modalities are merged to maximize classification performance. Our findings show how the multimodal model, using ResNet101 and VGG16 for image features and BERT for text data, performs much better compared to the unimodal methods. The highest Macro F1 Score of 0.87 is achieved by the multimodal approach, an indicator of a much better performance compared to the unimodal methods. Our work demonstrates the strength of multimodal fusion for meme classification tasks and improving the performance of classification by an appreciable margin.
