AB  - Surveys, performance evaluations, and basic HR analytics, the usual instruments for tracking employee well-being, are subjective, slow, and incapable of capturing quick emotional changes. Stress, weariness, or burnout have already had an impact on productivity and satisfaction; therefore, such reactive systems operate in such a way. The predictive approach, utilizing multimodal data and machine learning, surmounts these limitations by enabling immediate identification of emotions and enabling action in situations where emotions are at their peak. Long-term monitoring uses neural models with a unique architecture that includes facial expressions, voice tone, text sentiment, wearable data, and behavioral patterns. Predictive analytics anticipates possible dangers such as burnout, allowing time to act by balancing workloads, motivating staff to take mindfulness breaks, or having HR reach out. The experimental evaluation demonstrates considerable gains over previous methods in total emotion recognition (93.6%), burnout risk (47.2%), and stress detection (92.5%). It has major implications for improving workplace well-being.
