AB  - This research introduces NeuroAdapt, a revolutionary multimodal brain-computer interface (BCI) system intended for intelligent upper-limb rehab. It is the first system to integrate EEG and facial EMG signals for real-time detection of emotion, mental fatigue, and cognitive load. NeuroAdapt dynamically adapts therapy parameters—such as task difficulty, duration, and feedback style—based on these signals to enable individualized rehabilitation sessions. Since participation and adherence are often low among individuals with stroke or post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), the system is specifically tailored for them. NeuroAdapt combines a robotic assistive interface with a gamified training environment to support adaptive dual-task therapy addressing both motor and cognitive recovery. The system uses real-time machine learning models for mental state classification and reinforcement learning for adaptive control. It enables remote monitoring and supports home-based therapy by integrating physiological feedback into task delivery. Initial development and module testing confirm the feasibility of real-time adaptation using affordable wearable sensors. Future research will focus on system improvement and clinical trials.
