AB  - Voice-based emotion detection is gaining traction as a vital tool for building emotionally intelligent systems. While early research explored facial and multimodal cues, recent focus has shifted to speech and text due to their accessibility and potential for real-time applications. Transformer-based models like BERT and RoBERTa have significantly improved emotion classification from text, while deep learning architectures such as RNNs and MLPs are used to extract features from conversational audio. However, most systems focus solely on English and lack real-world integration. This paper reviews existing voice and speech-based emotion recognition techniques, emphasizing gaps in contextual awareness, language adaptability, and practical deployment. It also introduces a novel system that detects emotion from voice and triggers real-time alerts to supervisors, contributing to emotionally responsive environments in workplace settings.
