AB  - This paper presents an Embedded Wellness Support System designed to enhance mental well-being through expressive, multimodal human-machine interaction. The system is implemented on an Espressif Systems ESP32-S3 System-on-Chip (SoC) using the M5Stack Dial, integrating a circular Thin Film Transistor (TFT) display, microphone, speaker, and capacitive touch interface. It employs real-time facial animations, tactile response, and lightweight speech recognition to simulate empathetic communication without reliance on cloud computation. Unlike conventional digital assistants, the prototype provides combined visual, auditory, and tactile feedback to foster stigma-free emotional engagement. The architecture prioritizes affordability, portability, and full on-device processing, enabling operation in low-connectivity or resource-limited settings. Preliminary user evaluations indicate increased engagement, perceived emotional connection, and responsiveness during short-term interactions. These findings suggest that expressive embedded systems can bridge affective computing and assistive technology, offering a scalable pathway for stress management and emotional support applications in educational, therapeutic, and everyday wellness contexts.
