AB  - Emotion recognition (ER) has emerged as a pivotal field in affective computing, enabling natural human–machine interaction, mental health monitoring, and adaptive intelligent systems. This paper presents a comprehensive survey of multimodal emotion recognition, covering four primary modalities: speech, facial expressions, physiological signals, and text. We review benchmark datasets such as IEMOCAP, RAVDESS, DEAP, AMIGOS, GoEmotions, and DailyDialog, highlighting their signal characteristics, elicitation protocols, and annotation schemes. Classical handcrafted feature approaches (HOG, LBP) are contrasted with deep learning–based methods, including CNNs, LSTMs, 3D-CNNs, and vision transformers. For physiological signals, we emphasize wearable sensing technologies and their transition from controlled laboratory setups to real-world, continuous monitoring. The survey further explores fusion strategies, real-time inference challenges, and emerging directions such as transformer-based architectures and multimodal representation learning. By consolidating methodologies, datasets, and open challenges, this work provides a foundation for advancing robust, scalable, and context-aware emotion recognition systems.
