AB  - In today’s fast-paced digital world, rising levels of stress and mood disorders have become a major concern, impacting mental health and overall well-being. Addressing this challenge, the present study proposes an automated machine learning-based system for recognizing stress and mood using Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques. The system analyzes user-submitted textual data, transforming it into numerical representations using Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) vectorization. Multiple classifiers were evaluated, including Random Forest, Extra Trees, AdaBoost, and Logistic Regression. Among these, the Extra Trees classifier demonstrated the best performance with an accuracy of 90.68%, followed closely by Random Forest at 90.39%. Various pre-processing techniques—such as stopword removal, stemming, and lemmatization—contributed significantly to improved accuracy and reduced misclassification. Dimensionality reduction using Principal Component Analysis (PCA) helped maintain computational efficiency while preserving key features. The results demonstrate the effectiveness of ensemble models and NLP in identifying emotional states from text. This scalable, unbiased, and objective approach has immense potential in real-time mental health monitoring systems, such as chatbots, virtual therapy assistants, and workplace well-being platforms. Unlike conventional methods that rely on subjective assessments, this method offers a data-driven alternative for early diagnosis and intervention. Future improvements could include the incorporation of deep learning models like BERT and the use of multimodal data such as speech and physiological signals to enhance contextual understanding and prediction accuracy.
DO  - 10.1109/ICoICI65217.2025.11252594
