AB  - Pre-eclampsia, a leading pregnancy complication, is strongly linked to chronic stress, yet current monitoring methods lack real-time, data-driven solutions. This paper proposes an Emotion-Aware IoT System that integrates multi-modal sensing (wearable ECG, voice, facial expressions) and AI analytics to predict pre-eclampsia risk through stress detection. Our hybrid architecture combines edge-based lightweight models (LSTM + CNN) for real-time stress classification (91% accuracy) with cloud-based risk prediction (AUC 0.84) using fused physiological (HRV, BP) and behavioral (vocal jitter, facial action units) features. Validated on the WESAD and CK+ datasets with synthetic augmentation, the system achieves <50 ms latency for alerts while maintaining explainability via SHAP values (HRV: 38% contribution). Key innovations include a pregnancy-optimized ensemble model outperforming single-modality approaches by 9% F1-score and GDPR-compliant anonymization for data privacy. Results demonstrate strong stress-pre-eclampsia correlation (r=0.58, p<0.001), enabling proactive clinical interventions. This work bridges IoT, AI, and maternal healthcare, offering a scalable framework for early risk detection.
DO  - 10.1109/ACROSET66531.2025.11280987
