AB  - Suicide is an ongoing global problem in general, but especially among young people and disadvantaged groups, and a mental health crisis. Leveraging advances in natural language processing (NLP) and deep learning (DL), more researchers are developing AI systems for the early detection and intervention. This paper is a literature review ofthe results from 26 recent studies that used multimodal data - such as social media text, speech, facial expressions, and physiological signals - for suicide prevention. Review subjects of this review ranged from text- based suicide speech detection, emotion-aware chatbots, clinical data-driven risk prediction, to multimodal emotion analysis. Methods such as BERT, ELECTRA, Word2Vec, CNN, LSTM and graph-based attention model have been applied to understand unstructured mental health signals. There are a few works which pair transformer models with conversation AI systems (e.g., Dialog- GPT) for online response generation. While emphasizing performance improvement, the literature reviewed also touches on multilingual constraints, unexplainability, clinical validation gaps, and ethical concerns over data privacy and deployment. Looking forward, the paper addresses hybrid AI models, cross-modal fusion, real- time deployment, and culturally friendly datasets for a more effective suicide prevention technology.
