AB  - Depression detection remains a crucial challenge, especially in patients with severe physical disorders. To predict sadness from human conversations, we provide a unique multimodal strategy in this study that combines emotion detection and lip-reading approaches. Leveraging advances in deep learning, our model integrates Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN) for lip reading and mini-Exception-based emotion recognition for enhanced accuracy. We address limitations in existing models by simplifying the training procedure, introducing variable-length augmentation, and improving generalization to varying sequence lengths. Our experimental results on large datasets demonstrate significant performance gains, achieving state-of-the-art accuracy levels. The proposed approach holds promise for automatic, uniform, and reliable classification of signs of depression, contributing to improved patient care and well-being.
