AB  - Emotion recognition through facial expressions and gesture analysis has emerged as a key area of research in computer vision and human-computer interaction. The ability to automatically detect and classify human emotions has wide-ranging applications, from enhancing user experiences to improving mental health monitoring and surveillance systems. This project builds on this background to develop a comprehensive system for recognizing emotions and gestures in both humans and animals. The system integrates facial emotion recognition, hand gesture interpretation, and animal emotion detection into a single platform. Using advanced deep learning models and computer vision libraries, the system classifies facial expressions into categories such as happiness, sadness, anger, fear, surprise, and neutral. The gesture recognition component interprets hand signals to convey messages like “need help” or “need water.” An innovative aspect of this work is the extension of emotion recognition to animals, particularly focusing on species like dogs and monkeys. Implemented primarily in Python, the system leverages libraries such as OpenCV and DeepFace. By combining these different modalities of nonverbal communication analysis, the project presents a versatile solution with potential applications in human-computer interaction, mental health monitoring, security, and animal welfare.
