AB  - The strong connection between music and human emotion allows mood-based recommendation systems to provide therapeutic listening experience that benefits well-being and reduces stress, malaise, and depression. Unfortunately, traditional systems rely completely on previous listening data which means they cannot account for a user’s real-time emotional states and contexts. The work proposes a multimodal emotion-aware music recommendation system that integrates visual and textual emotional cues using an attention-based fusion to create highly tailored recommendations for emotional states. Facial expressions are collected in real-time using a web camera and are analyzed with an EfficientNetB0 model to classify the user into seven emotion families, while song lyrics are processed using a transformer-based RoBERTa model to extract sentiment. The features are then fused into a composite emotional profile of the user and are passed to a hybrid recommendation engine that employs a mixture of content-based filtering, collaborative filtering, and emotion-based matching to recommend songs from a multilingual content. The system achieved a 92.7% emotion recognition accuracy and a 0.93 NDCG@10, which was a 5.3% increase over single-modality systems, with an inference time of 24.2 ms allowing for real-time processing. User studies indicated a 43.8% increase in user satisfaction, a 55.2% increase in the emotional alignment of suggestions, and a 29.4% increase in the relevance of recommended suggestions. The proposed work represents an effective method of providing computationally efficient therapeutic recommendations through music.
DO  - 10.1109/ICoICI65217.2025.11252576
