AB  - Real-time recognition of sadness is an important issue in affective computing because it is a subtle facial expression and evolves over time. The paper introduces SadNet which is a new Deep Recurrent Emotion Dynamics Model which combines spatial, time-based, and physiologically aware features in the constant detection of sadness. SadNet is a system that jointly trains a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) to extract spatial patterns of muscle deformation of key facial areas with a bidirectional Long short-term memory (Bi-LSTM) network to predict the temporal dynamics of emotional state. The attention mechanism is weighted by An Action Unit (AU) to highlight the physiologically relevant muscles related to sadness, whereas optical flow and thermal differentials images are used to detect the subtle low intensity motions. This model makes use of cross-subject generalization through transfer learning using neutral baselines, which achieves individual-level and effective emotion mapping. Using AffectNet and FER2013 datasets, SadNet got an accuracy of more than 95 per cent with a real-time inference latency of 0.125 seconds per frame. The studies on ablation verify that temporal modeling, attention weighting, and multimodal fusion are effective. The suggested framework offers a physiologically based, temporally consistent, and computationally efficient real-time sadness recognition solution allowing it to be used in human-computer interaction, mental health, and affect-aware robotics.
DO  - 10.1109/SCEECS68810.2026.11429762
