AB  - This study aims to identify and analyze trends, techniques, and findings from recent research on predicting student stress and academic performance using machine learning. A Systematic Literature Review (SLR) was conducted following the PRISMA protocol, focusing on articles published between 2019 and 2025 from Scopus and other academic databases. Twenty relevant studies were reviewed, covering algorithm types, data preprocessing, feature types, and model performance. Random Forest, Support Vector Machine (SVM), and Neural Networks emerged as the most frequently used algorithms. Predictive features included psychological, behavioral, and physiological data collected from standardized instruments (e.g., DASS-42, PHQ-9), wearable biosensors, and facial expression analysis. Common preprocessing steps included text cleaning, tokenization, normalization, and dimensionality reduction. To handle class imbalance, most studies implemented SMOTE or undersampling techniques. Model optimization was typically conducted using grid search or Bayesian optimization, with some models achieving up to 97% accuracy. Geographically, India, China, and Indonesia were the most active contributors. Research was categorized into stress prediction (10 studies), academic performance prediction (5), and combined models (5). This review provides valuable insights into the use of intelligent systems for improving student well-being and academic success and highlights the need for explainable AI and multimodal data integration in future predictive modeling.
DO  - 10.1109/ICIC68054.2025.11309547
