AB  - Integration of large language models (LLMs) and multimodal interfaces is transforming the way machines interpret and react to the emotions of humans. This article describes a real-time application that determines the affective state of users by integrating their facial expressions, the vocal prosody, and the low-fidelity physiological data and creates contextually aware, sympathetic responses with the help of a built-in LLM. With cues combined between modalities rather than relying on one source, we will reduce the misclassification of the cues and gain a greater situational depth. We use small neural encoders to generate modality-specific feature and attention-based fusion layer to weighs the best trusted signals at any given moment. The simplified classifier is used to map fused representations with emotion labels, which guide the response planner of the LLM; the LLM will generate according to a given emotion and recent conversation history to generate a coherent and appropriate response. We use human-in-the-loop feedback based on reinforcement learning to optimize the phrasing and timing of responses to achieve improved naturalness. Controlled simulations and user studies were done to evaluate the system with the accuracy of recognition, relevance of response, perceived empathy and latency of interaction. Our multimodal pipeline was more accurate at detection than unimodal baselines, and generated more interesting and comprehensible responses to participants. Notably, the architecture is also efficient and allows to be deployed on edge devices and live applications. The piece shows that integrating LLMs into multimodal, emotion-responsive interfaces can generate more human-friendly interactions- helpful in tutoring systems, mental health support, and customer service- and points to trade-offs related to design responses between responsiveness, privacy, and interpretability. The future applications will be focused on personalization, strong privacy protection, and emotion modeling cross-culturally. Thus, the extraction of features in real-time may be achieved for segmented speech. In experimental settings, we find that emotional understanding accuracy increases by 12.1% over the baseline, and hybrid prompts that admit explicit keywords but also other implicit embeddings. Appropriate applications for this system are adaptive dialogue systems, social robotics akin to GPT-3. 5 language-to-language and trusted educational interfaces, where data is organised. Results of the newly developed EGS score are correlated to human expert ratings on 89% of the test cases.
