AB  - This paper presents a novel approach for detecting Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) using a Stochastic Transformer architecture specifically designed to process video sequence data. Utilizing the ‘PTSD-in-the-wild’ dataset, which consists of 613 interviews from individuals with and without PTSD, the model efficiently captures spatio-temporal features through 3D convolutional tubelet embedding and stochastic layers to enhance generalization and reduce overfitting. Key innovations include the use of a stochastic LWTA layer within the Transformer, allowing the model to perform with high accuracy (0.91), recall (0.92), and F1-score (0.90) compared to a baseline model. This study highlights the promising applications of advanced machine learning algorithms, particularly deep learning, in analyzing video data to aid in the diagnosis of mental health conditions in clinical settings.
