AB  - Mental health challenges among students and staff in Malaysian educational institutions are growing, yet access to formal counselling remains limited. This paper presents AIMI, a multimodal emotion-aware conversational AI designed to deliver culturally sensitive and anonymous support through voice, text, and facial interaction. AIMI integrates natural language processing tailored for Bahasa Melayu and English, a Depression-Anxiety-Stress (DAS) classification engine, a semantic query module informed by certified counsellors, and a responsive avatar interface. The system was developed using an Agile Iterative Development Model and tested through both technical validation and user acceptance testing (UAT). The BERT-TFIDF-CNN classifier trained on the MyDAS corpus achieved a macro-averaged F1-score of 0.88 across four emotional categories. UAT with 87 students demonstrated strong usability (92%), trust (88.5%), and cultural alignment (93.1%). The results indicate that AIMI preserves classifier accuracy while improving emotional engagement through voice, text, and facial interaction. AIMI provides a modular, privacy-focused digital mental health support system that aligns with Malaysia's Mental Health Strategic Plan 2020-2025. Its design responds to the unique linguistic, cultural, and institutional context of Malaysian higher education, offering a scalable first-line support tool for emotional well-being.
DO  - 10.1109/AiDAS67696.2025.11213678
