AB  - Facial expression recognition (FER) serves as an essential tool for assessing students' psychological and emotional states in educational environments, particularly within university mental health and ideological education contexts. Although the basic categories of facial expressions are limited, subtle muscle movements and partial occlusions make the recognition task more challenging. Recently, large-scale vision-language models such as CLIP have demonstrated remarkable potential across a wide range of tasks, setting new benchmarks for multimodal learning. However, compared with conventional vision tasks, their performance in FER remains relatively limited, mainly due to the subtle and dynamic nature of facial expressions and the weak alignment between visual cues and textual semantics in such fine-grained emotion tasks. To address this issue, we propose an enhanced CLIP-based framework that leverages prompt tuning and occlusion-aware design to improve adaptability and robustness in FER tasks. Specifically, we adopt CLIP with prompt tuning as the core framework to adapt the semantic space of textual prompts to nuanced emotional categories, enabling more discriminative and context-aware text representations. On the visual side, we incorporate facial landmarks as sparse geometric priors and design a cross-attention fusion module to integrate landmark-guided features with CLIP's visual tokens, enhancing its ability to capture subtle facial cues and maintain stability under occlusion. Experimental results on multiple public FER benchmarks indicate that our method effectively improves generalization and robustness under occlusion.
