AB  - In recent years, many studies have sought to assess an individual's psychological state and depressive symptoms by analyzing facial information, which can be extracted from static images or videos. However, the process of collecting images and videos in a controlled laboratory environment is both time-consuming and labor-intensive. Consequently, natural scene data, such as daily life recordings or video blogs (vlogs), hold significant research value due to their convenient data acquisition and high ecological validity. Based on this advantage, this study proposes a deep learning method for the automatic detection of depression by analyzing facial features in vlogs. A channel convolution attention block is introduced, and a 2D channel convolution model is constructed based on this block. The proposed method uses 2D separable deep convolution to extract feature information from both the temporal and feature channels. Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel attention is introduced to enhance the model's ability to express features across these two channels, thereby improving its focus on key features. This enhancement increases the representation of deep features and improves the model's capability to detect depression. The method is validated using the D-Vlog dataset, which contains a large number of strictly annotated vlogs. Experimental results show that the proposed method outperforms other methods in multiple performance indicators on the D-Vlog dataset, with a precision of 68.22%, a recall of 65.09% and an F1 score of 65.14%, which further proves the feasibility and effectiveness of depression detection based on daily life videos and provides new ideas for the early screening and automated assessment of depression.
