AB  - Identifying mental conditions, such as feelings, stress intensity, and cognitive states, has emerged as a crucial area in artificial intelligence. The ability to assess an individual's psychological state through non-invasive means like facial expressions and textual analysis offers wide-ranging applications in healthcare, security, and social media monitoring. With the rise of deep learning techniques, particularly CNN, systems have become increasingly adept at recognizing complex and subtle facial expressions across diverse populations. The study shows that these models correctly recognize the mental state including various health conditions. Simultaneously, advances in natural language processing have facilitated the extraction of emotional and psychological indicators from text. Early methods focused on sentiment analysis and lexicon-based approaches, although current models utilize deep learning frameworks to grasp more subtle emotions in text data. These approaches have proven particularly useful for analyzing social media posts, interviews, and conversational text to detect conditions like anxiety and depression. A growing body of work explores the integration of multimodal approaches, combining FER and NLP to improve mental state detection accuracy. This survey outlines the progress, issues, and upcoming developments in mental state identification, highlighting how AI-powered solutions could improve emotional evaluations. In the initial experiment it is found that transformer model BERT obtained highest accuracy of 69.28 % as compared to other models.
