AB  - Early predictions of mental health disorders is critical for timely intervention and improved outcomes. Traditional assessment methods relying on self-reports and periodic clinical evaluations are limited by subjectivity and infrequent sampling. In this study, we propose an integrated digital phenotyping framework that continuously monitors user emotions by fusing data from multiple sources: smartphone sensor data and web browsing behaviour, complemented by voice recognition and face detection. Our software uses advanced machine learning models to analyze data obtained via users to enable the detection of subtle emotional transitions and prevailing affective states. Based on these predictions, the solution helps users with personalized, just-in-time support interventions while securing data privacy through secure data-protecting protocols. Experimental evaluations indicate that multiple models achieve high predictive accuracy in classifying emotional states and detecting transitions with personalized models outperforming generalized approaches. This integrated approach not only enhances the granularity of mood assessment but also supports proactive mental health management, offering a scalable solution to expand traditional diagnostic methods.
