AB  - Assessment of pain intensity remains a significant challenge in healthcare. In recent years, significant advancements have been achieved in the field of automated pain detection. Modalities such as facial expression analysis [1], vocalisation patterns [2], and textual sentiment [3], each offer a unique insight into pain-related behaviour. This research presents a comparative investigation of three modalities - facial expressions, voice, and text - to perform pain detection and classification using state-of-the-art deep learning techniques. By identifying the most important landmarks, our proposed CNN-LSTM-Attention-based model achieved an impressive pain intensity classification score of 87.15%. In the audio domain, non-speech vocalisations (e.g., groans, cries) from the ASPVD dataset are converted into mel-spectograms and then fine-tuned using Audio Spectrogram Transformer (AST) to classify pain-related vocal patterns. This model achieved 80% accuracy in identifying non-speech vocalisation associated with pain. For textual data, pain-related sentiment was analysed using the EmoLit data set. By fine-tuning the RoBERTa transformer model, a sentiment analysis of emotions associated with pain allowed for a more accurate detection with a score of 95%. This comparative analysis highlights the unique strengths of each modality in pain intensity recognition. It also provides valuable information for multimodal fusion systems. The findings of this research aim to contribute to a greater understanding of developing more robust, clinically relevant and accurate pain recognition tools.
