AB  - Depression is a complex, multidimensional mental health disorder influenced by biological, psychological, and behavioral factors. Traditional diagnostic methods, such as clinical interviews and self-report questionnaires, remain limited by subjectivity and cultural bias. Recent advances in artificial intelligence (AI), wearable sensing, and immersive technologies have enabled objective, continuous, and multi- modal assessment of depression. This systematic review synthesizes findings from 24 peer-reviewed studies published between 2020 and 2024, examining the integration of physiological signals electroencephalography (EEG), electrocardiography (ECG), heart rate variability (HRV)with behavioral modalities such as speech, text, gaze tracking, and facial analysis, alongside augmented and virtual reality (AR/VR) frameworks for therapeutic engagement. The reviewed studies employed machine learning and deep learning architectures (CNNs, RNNs, transformers) and fusion strategies (early, intermediate, late) for multimodal data integration. Evidence indicates that multimodal fusion improves depression detection accuracy by 8–12% over unimodal systems. However, methodological gaps persist regarding dataset diversity, synchronization, clinical validation, and explainability. By consolidating trends in sensing, modeling, and AR/VR-based feedback, this review provides an evidence-based roadmap for the development of scalable, interpretable, and ethically aligned AI systems for next-generation mental health assessment and intervention.
