AB  - Mental health disorders are a major global public health concern and require novel strategies for diagnosis, treatment and long-term monitoring. This research proposes an integrative framework to address these challenges by incorporating intelligent methods in a modular addiction cohort analysis that uses some state-of-the-art AI techniques and combining them with on-demand treatment protocols and real time monitoring through immersive VR environments. The ultimate goal is to design emotion-aware VR therapy systems that can provide tailored, adaptive, and impactful mental health interventions. The system that is proposed integrates affective computing, AI-based sentiment analysis and behavior tracking with the Psychological Treatment Modules that are embedded in interactive VR scenarios. The system is continually measuring the user's emotional and cognitive state by multimodal sensors (e.g., eye-tracking, voice tone analysis, physiological data quantity e.g., heart rate, galvanic Skin response, and facial expression recognition). AI algorithms take this data and dynamically adapt therapeutic content in real time, adjusting environmental stimuli or guided therapy instructions or task difficulty levels based on the user's engagement and emotional responsiveness. The system architecture involves reinforcement learning and neural networks to optimize personalized therapy plans affected by users' psychological profile therapeutic goals. Nested within the VR simulations are cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT), mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR), and exposure therapy modules in a manner that creates safe, controlled therapeutic experiences. For instance, there is a depiction of phobia exposure and anxiety reduction scenarios in which can be progressively increased or decreased according to feedback from real-time affective loops. A combination that renders the treatment as intensive while at the same time clinically adaptive. A pilot trial with generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) and PTSD showed significant results in emotional regulation, adherence to the therapy program and long-term retention. Conversely, the emotion-aware system improved therapy adherence by over 30% compared to static VR therapy and, in this pilot study, was more effective than the static VR system at reducing anxiety symptoms of participants over time. Additionally, the use of real-time data analytics allows therapists to follow progress and tailor sessions as necessary on a remote basis without intervention, which can potentially provide sustainable solutions for under-resourced healthcare environments. The results only further underscore the prospects of combining AI and VR therapy for advances in modern mental health care.
