AB  - Multimodal content from social media offers critical insights into emotional states, serving as a cornerstone for the early detection of mental health issues such as depression and anxiety. However, existing multimodal models—typically combining CNNs for image processing and Transformers for text modeling—restrict cross-modal interactions to final semantic layers. This limitation overlooks fine-grained emotional features in intermediate features. To address this problem, we propose a Cross-Hierarchical Feature Interaction Transformer (CHFI) framework for social media analysis. CHFI constructs a novel multimodal interaction paradigm that jointly models three types of features: local visual features from intermediate vision encoder layers, grammatical dependencies and semantic features from intermediate text encoder layers, and features from fusion-layer intermediates. By enabling dynamic cross-modal interactions across these hierarchical features, CHFI fully leverages fine-grained emotional information in multimodal data. This enhances the reliability of emotional tendency analysis, providing a more precise technical foundation for monitoring psychological states.
