AB  - Mental health conditions such as stress, anxiety, and depression are increasingly common among university students, yet stigma, resource constraints, and reliance on self-disclosure limit early detection. Advances in artificial intelligence (AI) enable multimodal approaches that integrate self-reports, academic records, behavioural traces, physiological signals, and facial or vocal features to enhance prediction and scalability. This paper presents a scoping review of multimodal AI for early detection of student mental health conditions in the UK. Ten primary studies were identified, utilising methods from logistic regression and support vector machines to develop advanced models, including LightGBM, XGBoost, deep learning, and cross-modal transformers. Multimodal integration consistently outperforms unimodal approaches, although challenges remain regarding interpretability, dataset diversity, validation, and ethical issues.
