AB  - In healthcare monitoring, emotions are vital, particularly for the early identification of neurological and stress-related disorders. The accuracy and robustness of traditional emotion recognition techniques are limited in real-world scenarios because they frequently rely on a single modality, such as physiological signals or facial expressions. In this study, we present a wearable-assisted emotion recognition system that uses multimodal deep learning on facial, EEG, and ECG signals to analyse emotional states in-depth. Lightweight wearable technology is integrated into the suggested framework to gather physiological data, and camera-enabled devices are used to record facial micro expressions. These diverse modalities are fused using a hybrid deep learning model, which improves the detection of subtle emotional biomarkers linked to stress, anxiety, Parkinson's disease, and Alzheimer's disease. Explainable AI techniques are used to emphasize the role of each signal in the decision-making process, ensuring interpretability. Experiments on publicly accessible multimodal emotion datasets show that the suggested system outperforms unimodal methods in terms of recognition accuracy. According to the findings, the suggested framework is well suited for integration into next-generation smart healthcare systems because it offers substantial promise for early neurological disorder diagnosis, personalized healthcare, and remote patient monitoring.
