AB  - Through the use of deep learning techniques that combine auditory and visual data gathered from human behaviour, scientists have made significant strides in the identification of emotions. This strategy can produce more accurate and precise predictions by utilising a variety of communication indicators, including spoken language, body and facial signals, and vocal tone. Improved emotion recognition accuracy is crucial for human-centric applications including affective computing, mental health monitoring, and humancomputer interaction. There are still some unresolved concerns with real-time processing, cross-modal representation learning, modal fusion techniques, and data synchronisation. We assess the capacity of several deep learning networks, such as CNNs, RNNs, and Transformers models, to extract information from synchronised visual face data (lip movements and facial microexpressions) and aural signals (pitch, tone, and speech rhythm). Through experiments conducted on benchmark datasets RAVDESS as well as CREMA-D and AFEW with accuracy metrics and latency variables under varied noisy background settings, the study compares deep learning models. To improve system interpretability and efficiency performance, the study design gives particular emphasis to feature-level integration as well as attention-based alignment strategies. When resources are scarce, the research team uses the Raspberry Pi 4 and NVIDIA Jetson Nano to evaluate experimental installations to confirm edge capabilities. This study presents future research prospects centred on adaptive training across modalities, selfsupervised learning, and ethical guidelines for AI systems with compassion capabilities. It also provides particular insights into multi-modality emotion detection systems.
