AB  - In an age of smartphones and digital healthcare, ensuring that patients stick to their medication plans is a constant challenge, especially for those with chronic illnesses and long- term therapies. Emotional distress, often felt by these patients, plays a significant role in non-compliance. This can make treatment ineffective and lead to serious health issues. Depression, one of the most common mental health disorders, is widespread yet often goes undiagnosed. It can easily be overlooked, particularly when patients don’t openly discuss their emotional struggles. This hidden suffering can greatly impact recovery and overall well-being. Traditional healthcare systems often ignore the mental aspects of patient care, concentrating mainly on physical symptoms. The lack of regular checks for emotional and mental health makes the situation worse and delays needed interventions. There is an increasing need for effective ways to identify signs of depression early, track emotional decline, and provide timely mental health support within larger healthcare systems.
