AB  - Good mental health is vital for societal progress, but mental health challenges, exacerbated by global events like pandemics, have grown. Children with mental disorders face hurdles in education, work, and personal life, often lacking access to treatment. Advancements in technology, particularly AI, have sparked interest in automating mental illness detection. This article systematically reviews nine common childhood disorders, focusing on automated identification via physiological signals. It discusses signal analysis, feature engineering, and decision-making processes, highlighting benefits, challenges, and future directions. The paper also covers dataset details, validation methods, and key findings while posing research questions on signal availability and interpretability. Our focus in this paper revolves around the automated identification of these disorders through physiological signals. We also delve into a thorough discussion of signal analysis, feature engineering, and decision-making processes, highlighting their advantages, potential future directions, and challenges as reported in previous research on children's mental disorders. The article provides a comprehensive overview of the dataset description, validation techniques, extracted features, and decision- making models employed in these studies. Furthermore, it raises open research questions regarding signal availability, uncertainty, interpretability, and hardware implementation resources for signal analysis and machine or deep learning models. Lastly, the study's key findings are summarized in the conclusion section.
