AB  - As mental health issues continue to rise and significantly affect human quality of life, sometimes even leading to suicide, preventive and mitigative actions are urgently needed. Affective computing, supported by artificial intelligence (AI), offers promising tools such as Facial Expression Recognition (FER) for assessing mental health conditions. FER can detect and classify facial expressions associated with mental health symptoms, including depression, autism, and anxiety. The implementation of FER in healthcare settings depends on the readiness and capacity of the health service providers. Key aspects to consider include system performance and validity, the availability of infrastructure and devices, adherence to ethical protocols, and the necessity of clinical trials before broader adoption. This study aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the requirements for implementing FER, explore its limitations and challenges, compare various techniques, and discuss the potential of multimodal approaches for enhanced mental health assessment.
