AB  - Depression has become a widespread mental health challenge within academic communities around the globe. This study proposes a comprehensive, multimodal framework for detecting depressive symptoms among university students and staff by leveraging advancements in Natural Language Processing (NLP), Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (AI-ML), and Computer Vision. The framework integrates data collected from structured surveys, video interviews, and user-generated textual content from social media. Features drawn from linguistic, visual, and behavioral domains are processed through a combination of deep learning and traditional machine learning models. We detail the end-to-end pipeline—from data acquisition and preprocessing to multimodal fusion, model training, and performance evaluation. The proposed system demonstrates strong predictive performance and holds promise as a scalable, proactive tool for mental health monitoring in academic settings. This paper further explores methodological design, implementation challenges, ethical considerations and future research directions.
