AB  - This study proposes MindSync, a novel multimodal deep learning framework for enhanced depression detection that integrates audio, video, and EEG data. In the preprocessing stage, modalityspecific techniques are applied: Mel-Frequency Cepstral Coefficients (MFCCs) are used for noise reduction and feature extraction from audio signals; MobileNet V2, MobileViT, and OpenFace are employed for facial feature extraction from video data; and ShallowConvNet, Power Spectral Density (PSD), and Fractal Dimensions are utilized to extract neurological features from EEG signals. A Light-Weight Self-Refinement Attention (LSRA) mechanism is introduced to refine and fuse the extracted features, selecting the most relevant indicators for depression classification. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed model achieves superior performance, with an accuracy of 99.11 %, a balanced F1score of 98.49 %, precision of 97.89 %, and sensitivity of 98.20 %. Furthermore, the model demonstrates rapid training efficiency, completing training within 10.28 seconds, and maintains low false positive (1.00 %) and false negative (0.92 %) rates. Extensive validation confirms the robustness and effectiveness of MindSync, highlighting its potential as a reliable tool for objective mental health evaluation.
