AB  - The research on smile authenticity is based on the Duchenne smile pattern, often associated with genuine happiness. The Facial action coding system (FACS) is the universal standard for identifying the muscles. The co-activation of Facial Action unit muscles-AU6 (cheek raiser) and AU12 (lip-corner puller) create Duchenne smile pattern. The other genuineness indicators are naso-labial folds, eye sockets crinkling and the lines around and under eyes. The AU6 - Cheek raiser muscle movement in smile is driven by orbicularis oculi muscle, especially the orbital and palpebral sections around the outer eye area. This causes the cheeks to lift and produces small wrinkles at the eye corners. In contrast, AU12 (Lip Corner Puller muscle) is controlled by the zygomaticus major muscle, which draws the corners of the mouth upward and outward to form a smile. Since people can fake this pattern, relying on AU6 and AU12 does not always suffice. Therefore, we introduce a Multi-Modal Temporal Synchrony (MMTS) approach. MMTS looks at how different signals—such as multiple facial movements, head motion, eye gaze, and voice rise and fall together over time. Instead of checking a particular muscle’s activity, this approach measures how well these different signals are coordinated. This uses a new Multi-Modal Temporal Synchrony Index (MM-TSI). MM-TSI coordinates rise–peak–offset patterns typical of spontaneous smiles and captures the natural timing and rhythm that tends to occur in genuine smiles. By combining these synchrony features with machine learning models, our method offers a more trustworthy way to distinguish real smiles from posed ones. The temporal sequence of features and MM-TSI values are passed through a Bi-Directional Long Short-Term Memory (BiLSTM) to model long-range dependencies. Attention mechanisms are applied to assign modality-specific weights dynamically. This multimodal view makes smile detection more robust and useful for applications in human–computer interaction, mental health, and social robotics.
