AB  - Depression is a mental health disorder characterized by mental instability, such as mood swings and deep sadness, as well as a loss of interest in things. This condition can have serious consequences, such as loss of motivation and even suicide, if not detected early. Therefore, early detection of signs of depression is important in order to provide appropriate intervention and support. This study aims to detect depression through Indonesianlanguage tweets on the social media $\mathbf{X}$ using models Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), and hybrid deep learning CNN+LSTM, and LSTM+CNN. Text data was extracted using Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) and expanded with Word2Vec as a feature expansion technique to enrich the semantic representation of text. The dataset used consisted of 24,779 labeled data and a total corpus similarity of 14,704 used in feature expansion. This study focuses on evaluating performance efficiency of the model in several scenarios, including a comparison between the Word2Vec and FastText expansion methods. The results show that feature expansion using Word2Vec provides the best performance and is superior to FastText. The experimental results show that the highest accuracy was achieved by the CNN-LSTM hybrid model at 81.48 %, precision of 80.54 %, recall of 72.07 %, and an F 1 -score of 73.95 %, while the LSTM-CNN model achieved an accuracy of 80.97 %, with a precision of 79.97 %, recall of 71.52 %, and an F1-score of $\mathbf{7 3. 3 0 \%}$ demonstrating the efficiency of a combination of hybrid deep learning architectures in detection of depression based on Indonesian language-based multimodal data.
