AB  - In response to the increasing psychological burden faced by employees in digitalized and high-pressure work environments, organizations are seeking new approaches to monitor mental well-being beyond traditional surveys and retrospective interviews. Despite rapid advances in multimodal emotion recognition (MER) technology, its application in human resource management remains fragmented, particularly in understanding the linkage between employee affective states and behavioral outcomes such as engagement, burnout, and turnover. This study addresses this gap by developing a deep learning-based MER system that integrates facial expressions, vocal cues, and textual communication to identify six core emotions and assess emotional stability. Based on four weeks of real-world data from 120 employees in a technology company, the system demonstrated superior classification performance (F1-score = 0.81) and revealed significant correlations between emotional profiles and key organizational behavior indicators. Regression and classification models confirmed the predictive power of happiness, sadness, and emotional instability for engagement, burnout risk, and turnover intention. These findings underscore the feasibility and utility of emotion-aware HR analytics, offering both a technical framework and empirical evidence for integrating real-time emotional sensing into proactive mental health support and organizational decision-making.  © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
