AB  - Depression, the most common psychological disorder, is a major contributor to suicide. Medical research reveals that physical illnesses and personality traits significantly influence depression risk by inducing varying levels of psychological stress and emotional response. However, existing depression detection approaches face three major limitations: first, they rely solely on behavioral features while neglecting the influence of disease and personality information; second, they fail to effectively capture the spatial relationships among features across different time points; and third, conventional multimodal fusion methods struggle to fully exploit the complementary information between modalities, often resulting in redundant data. To address these issues, this paper introduces the NEUD multimodal depression dataset, which includes facial video, audio, gait, personality data, and disease data from 118 individuals. Additionally, the correlation between disease and depression severity was examined with the Kruskal–Wallis H test, while personality traits’ association with depression severity was analyzed using multiple linear regression. Finally, a multimodal depression recognition framework, TransGCL, is proposed. This framework incorporates a GCN module consisting of fully connected graphs and a trainable attenuation matrix, along with a regularization-based orthogonal tri-axis multimodal fusion approach. In addition, the framework integrates disease types and personality traits to enhance the accuracy of depression recognition. Experimental results demonstrate that the classification task achieved an accuracy of 74 %, while the mean absolute error for the regression task reached 4.37, validating the effectiveness of the proposed model and method. This study offers novel insights and methodologies for depression detection, providing valuable references for future research. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
