AB  - Depression, a mental illness, is often overlooked and has high rates of misdiagnosis. Numerous global researchers are investigating employing deep learning for depression detection, assisting psychologists in the clinical diagnosis of depression. However, human emotional expression is inherently arbitrary and complex. One of the common challenges in current research is how to effectively leverage and integrate depression related features from multiple modalities to improve the accuracy of depression detection. In response to these issues, this paper proposes a multimodal hierarchical transformer enriched by temporal features and the CTC loss model (MTC-Former). MTC-Former embraces inputs from three modalities (audio, text, and video) to enhance its detection accuracy. By considering the unique linguistic structure of human emotional expression, MTC-Former performs feature extractions in a three-stage (frame, phoneme, and word) hierarchical architecture. The Multi-modal Attention Fusion (MAF) Module is adopted in each stage to integrate multimodal features, overcoming the dual-modality input limitation of the cross-attention mechanism. Meanwhile, the Temporal Feature Fusion Module (TF-FM) is proposed to fuse temporal answering features of the interview, providing prior knowledge and emotional continuance for depression detections. MTC-Former also combines CTC and Cross-Entropy losses to compute local and global losses, enhancing the hierarchical feature extraction process in intermediate stages. In experiments, MTC-Former reaches an MF1 score of 0.926 on the multimodal English-language depression detection dataset DAIC-WOZ, demonstrating superior effectiveness compared to other mainstream models. MTC-Former also shows remarkable performances on the English-language dataset E-DAIC and the Chinese-language dataset CMDC, proving its robustness across diverse cultural and language conditions. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
