AB  - Emotion classification in social media text is essential for applications in mental health monitoring, sentiment analysis, and human-computer interaction. We evaluated the performance of deep learning models, including convolutional neural networks (CNNs), long short-term memory networks (LSTMs), and bidirectional encoder representations from transformers (BERT), for emotion classification. Using a dataset comprising texts from Daily Dialog, Emotion-Stimulus, and ISEAR, we preprocessed data through tokenization, text normalization, and word embedding techniques. The BERT model presented the highest accuracy (77%) by effectively capturing contextual information, while CNN (76%) and LSTM (21%) models exhibited limitations in handling nuanced emotional expressions. The study results highlight the importance of context-aware models in improving emotion classification from short texts. Multimodal approaches need to be integrated with speech and facial expressions to enhance classification accuracy. © 2025 IEEE.
