AB  - Background: Artificial intelligence (AI) technology has increasingly attracted attention in the field of psychological emotion recognition. Numerous systematic reviews and meta-analyses have been published on this topic. However, Existing research often focuses on a single aspect, such as themes, methods, or time periods. This makes it difficult for researchers to gain a comprehensive understanding of the overall progress and research hotspots in this area. Objective: This study aims to explore the development, hotspots, and emerging trends in AI-related research in psychological emotion recognition through bibliometric analysis. Method: The Web of Science Core Collection database was selected as the data source. Relevant literature on AI in psychological emotion recognition was retrieved and collected. All extracted data were imported into CiteSpace and VOSviewer for bibliometric and knowledge map analysis. Results and Conclusions: 1. A total of 648 articles were included. The annual publication volume shows a fluctuating upward trend. China is the leading country in publication output, with the Chinese Academy of Sciences and its affiliated universities as the most active institutions. The authors are relatively dispersed across the field. 2. Analysis of co-citation networks and journals indicates that research primarily focuses on databases, neural network architectures, and emotions within AI for psychological emotion recognition. 3. High-frequency keywords revolve around eight main areas: diseases, functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI), anterior cingulate cortex, deep learning, random forests, emotion computing, attention, and cognitive control. Facial expressions, deep learning, and the brain are the most prominent research hotspots. 4. Future trends are likely to include multimodal emotion analysis and solutions for specific mental health issues. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
