AB  - As artificial intelligence rapidly develop, the combination of intelligent algorithms and mental health care provides multiple research ideas for the assessment of children's mental health status. To address the issues of low computational efficiency and easy gradient explosion of recurrent neural networks, the study constructed a prediction model for children's mental health by integrating the bidirectional long short-term memory network and the cross-attention module. First, optimize the long short-term memory network and use the bidirectional long short-term memory network to extract features from facial images and voices. Then, the cross-attention module is introduced for multimodal information fusion, thereby enhancing the accuracy of the mental health prediction model for young children. Furthermore, experiments were set up to verify the performance of the mental health prediction model for young children. Experiments were set up in the EmoReact dataset for verification. The experiments showed that the performance indicators of the prediction model proposed in the study were the highest, with the accuracy rate, precision rate, F1, R2 and recall rate being 0.902, 0.911, 0.904, 0.905 and 0.897 respectively. In addition, comparative experiments were conducted in eight basic emotions. The findings denoted that the accuracy rate of the prediction model proposed in the study was at least 0.877 and at most 0.900 under different emotions. In conclusion, the mental health prediction model for young children proposed in the research has improved the accuracy and stability of the prediction, and at the same time has good generalization ability, with broad application prospects.  © 2026 SPIE.
