AB  - Depression can severely impact an individual’s mental health and overall quality of life if not treated promptly. Physiological studies have revealed significant differences in audio and facial expressions between individuals with depression and those without. Multimodal approaches to depression detection, which integrate information from multiple modalities, often exhibit superior performance in comparison to single-modal methods. However, there are still some challenges to hinder effective fusion, including semantic discrepancies and distribution inconsistencises across modalities. To address these issues, we propose a disentangled representation learning framework, designed to decompose homogeneous, heterogeneous, and noise information within multi-modal features, thereby reducing the adverse effects of distribution mismatches. Specifically, the first step involves the utilization of a convolutional neural network and two types of transformer encoder to extract features from audio and facial modalities. Considering the existence of noise signals, feature disentanglement decomposes these features into distinct components, with various regularization losses imposed to constrain the distance relationships among the homogeneous, heterogeneous, and noisy representations. Finally, a cross graph fusion mechanism is introduced to facilitate context-aware fusion across modalities, further enhancing the representation learning and generalization capabilities of the model. Experiments conducted on the D-Vlog and LMVD datasets demonstrate that our method outperforms existing state-of-the-art approaches across key metrics, validating its effectiveness. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM.
